      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1774197621934,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "-> prompts are the input instructions or queries given to a model\nto guide its output.\n\n-> there are 2 types of text based prompts: static prompts and \ndynamic prompts.\n\n-> Prompt Template\nit is a structured way to create dynamic prompts by inserting\nvariable in to a predefined template.\nthis make the prompt resusable, flexible, and easy to manage.\n\n-> there is one doubt that come instantly to my  mind, we can \nuse f string so what is the use of this template,\nhere some solid reasons to use prompt template:\n \n 1. default validation (checks the code and enables deubugging)\n 2. Reusable\n 3. LangChain Ecosystem\n\n ",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "-> prompts are the input instructions or queries given to a model\nto guide its output.\n\n-> there are 2 types of text based prompts: static prompts and \ndynamic prompts.\n\n-> Prompt Template\nit is a structured way to create dynamic prompts by inserting\nvariable in to a predefined template.\nthis make the prompt resusable, flexible, and easy to manage.\n\n-> there is one doubt that come instantly to my  mind, we can \nuse f string so what is the use of this template,\nhere some solid reasons to use prompt template:\n \n 1. default validation (checks the code and enables deubugging)\n 2. Reusable\n 3. LangChain Ecosystem\n\n ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
